# MD-JSON Synchronization System: Agent Architecture

## Overview

This document outlines the distributed agent architecture for the MD-JSON synchronization system, following a modular approach where independent agents handle specific aspects of the synchronization process.

## Agent Types and Responsibilities

### Alpha Agent: File System Monitor
- Monitors file changes using intelligent event debouncing
- Handles directory watching with proper exclusion patterns
- Provides file change notifications to other agents
- Manages root directory monitoring

### Beta Agent: Content Transformer
- Handles Markdown to JSON conversion
- Ensures content structure preservation 
- Implements validation checks for data integrity
- Prevents destructive transformations

### Gamma Agent: Conflict Resolver
- Implements intelligent section-level merging
- Manages conflict resolution between formats
- Preserves historical content during merges
- Detects and prevents content reduction

### Delta Agent: Safety Manager
- Creates and manages versioned backups
- Implements transaction-based operations
- Handles lock file management
- Provides recovery mechanisms

### Epsilon Agent: Process Controller
- Manages process lifecycle
- Implements Cursor process detection
- Prevents multiple instances
- Handles graceful shutdown

## Communication Protocol

Agents communicate through a message bus that allows for:
- Event-based notifications
- Task delegation
- Status reporting
- Error handling

## Implementation Timeline

| Phase | Focus | Estimated Hours | Primary Agents |
|-------|-------|-----------------|----------------|
| 1 | Core Architecture | 1-2 hours | Epsilon |
| 2 | Synchronization Engine | 2-3 hours | Beta, Gamma |
| 3 | Safety Features | 1-2 hours | Delta |
| 4 | Monitoring System | 1-2 hours | Alpha |
| 5 | Integration & Testing | 2-3 hours | All |

## Parallel Processing Strategy

The system will implement parallel processing where safe, with the following constraints:
- File operations are serialized per file to prevent race conditions
- Content transformation can occur in parallel for different files
- Monitoring runs concurrently with transformation 
- Safety operations take priority and can block other operations